;;; theorem-library/riesz-markov-model.scm -- RIESZ-MARKOV, STAGE 1 (RM-1,
;;; 2026-10-10): the Cantor model K of a compact metric space and the map
;;; pi : K -> s.  Vocabulary: structure-library/riesz-markov-model.scm.
;;; Source: the user's notes-63, concrete form (b).  Helper prefix: rmm-.
;;; 69 theorems, every one `proven modulo 0'; no support, warrant or stamp.
;;;
;;; THE HEADLINES (s compact, dseq a dense sequence of s, K = RM-K(s, dseq)):
;;;   rmm-strong-separation  two different points lie strictly inside two
;;;                          DISJOINT balls of the family (outside the shells).
;;;   rmm-cantor-compact     the Cantor space {0,1}^NN is compact (countable
;;;                          Tychonoff, compact-countable-product, modulo 0).
;;;   rmm-k-closed           K is closed in the Cantor space;
;;;   rmm-k-compact          K, as a subspace (RM-KMS), is compact.
;;;   rmm-pi-spec / -value   pi(es) is THE point with address es (existence:
;;;                          rmm-address-exists, a convergent subsequence of
;;;                          realizers; uniqueness: rmm-address-unique, strong
;;;                          separation);  rmm-pi-fun: pi in FUN(K, PTS s).
;;;   rmm-pi-continuous      pi : RM-KMS(s, dseq) -> s is continuous.
;;;   rmm-pi-onto            pi is onto; rmm-pi-code: pi(RM-CODE(x)) = x.
;;;   rmm-cyl-open / -closed every cylinder RM-CYL(s, dseq, a, nb) is clopen in K;
;;;   rmm-cyl-base           an open set of K contains, around each of its points
;;;                          es, the cylinder of es of some length (rmm-cyl-self:
;;;                          es lies in it) -- every open set is a union of cylinders.
;;;   rmm-dense-seq-exists   a compact metric space with a point has a dense sequence.
;;;
;;; ROUTES.  The two-point space is {0,1} as a SUBSPACE of RR-MS (no new metric);
;;; it is sequentially compact by pigeonhole-infinite + subsequence-capture.
;;; Every "points near es agree with es on finitely many coordinates" comes from
;;; ONE induction (rmm-agree-near) over the continuity of the coordinate
;;; projections (product-projection-continuous) -- no estimate on the series
;;; that defines PRODUCT-METRIC is ever needed.  The converse direction (agreeing
;;; on long prefixes implies close) is used once, in rmm-cyl-base, through
;;; product-convergence-coordinatewise-bwd on a sequence.  Sequences by choice go
;;; through ONE generic lemma, rmm-nn-choice (the family is a variable,
;;; instantiated at a VNB-LAMBDA of SEPs), driven by rmm-choose-seq! / rmm-seq-at!.
;;; The cell "closure of X \ B_n" of the brief is RM-SHELL(n) = {y : r_n <= d(c_n, y)},
;;; a closed superset of it (rmm-shell-closed); uniqueness only needs that a point
;;; strictly inside B_n is outside it (rmm-inside-ball).
;;;
;;; LOAD WINDOW: [after theorem-library/ms-continuity-algebra, end).  The latest
;;; citation is ms-pts-is-set (ms-continuity-algebra); the next latest are
;;; compact-countable-product (tychonoff-proof), product-convergence-coordinatewise-bwd
;;; (product-convergence), compact-metric-is-separable (compact-separable-proof).
;;; Tactics: the dk- kit, push-not-h, prop, ineq, arith -- all load before any
;;; proof file.  No citer.  The vocabulary file needs only metric-topology,
;;; metric-subspace, product-metric, numeric-instances and nn-pairing; place it
;;; immediately before this file.  Library of both files: analysis.
;;; No top-level macro is used: the file may be compiled.

